// contentStatus.js -- MSL machine-readable module pipeline status (companion to
// moduleTiers.js). Single source of truth for "has this module completed the full
// writer -> Pass-2 blind adversarial audit -> glossary/interview-question harvest"
// pipeline. moduleTiers.js answers WHICH modules exist and their interview tier;
// this file answers WHERE each one currently is in the content-quality pipeline.
// docs/BACKLOG.md remains the narrative history -- this file is the queryable
// CURRENT STATE, generated by seeding every TIER_S/TIER_A moduleId from
// moduleTiers.js (so no module can be silently dropped) and only overriding to a
// real status where independently verified.
//
// Status values: 'unclassified' | 'pending' | 'in_progress' | 'clean' -- see GSL's
// contentStatus.js header for the full definitions (identical convention, both labs).
//
// HARD RULE (CLAUDE.md's 2026-07-10 Recordkeeping section): never set a module's
// status to 'clean' from memory of a prior summary sentence -- only in the same edit
// where you've just personally verified it. This file was seeded 2026-07-11 in the
// exact session that caught BACKLOG.md's own "S-tier-complete" overclaim (`generalization`
// was never actually touched despite the closing sentence implying it was) -- so on
// principle, Classical ML batch 1/2's other modules (linear_regression,
// logistic_regression, regularization, trees, random_forest, gradient_boosting,
// class_imbalance) are left 'unclassified' here too, even though BACKLOG.md's prose
// describes them as done -- re-verify before flipping them to 'clean', don't just
// transcribe the prose claim into structured form.
//
// SCHEMA RULE (identical convention to GSL's contentStatus.js, keep both in sync):
// any entry with status: 'clean' MUST include a `verifiedBy` string field -- an
// IST timestamp of when the check actually ran, a re-runnable check (exact grep +
// file, exact command + exit status, exact file:line), and what it specifically
// confirmed. Not optional, not satisfied by the bare word "verified". A 'clean'
// entry with no real `verifiedBy` is worse than 'unclassified' -- it's a false
// claim of confidence, not an absence of one.

